A stepper cell answers the selector its own control sends.

NSStepper.m declares a private category on NSStepperCell,

    - (NSImage *) _stepUpImage;
    - (NSImage *) _stepDownImage;

and -[NSStepper sizeToFit] sends both. NSStepperCell implements NEITHER, and it never could:
it draws both arrows procedurally through NSGraphicsStyleForView drawStepperButtonInRect: and owns
no images at all. So our own code sends a method we never wrote, and any application that lays out
a stepper takes an unrecognized selector.

THAT IS NOT A CRASH, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED. The raise is caught by whatever is building the UI,
so the application simply stops partway and the log shows nothing but one line. iTerm2 raises it
while decoding its Preferences nib and gets NO PREFERENCES WINDOW AT ALL, the same shape as #254
where one caught selector cost MoneyMoney the same window.

The cell now answers nil for both, because nil is the truth: there are no images. sizeToFit treats
nil as nothing to measure and leaves the control at the size it has, rather than computing from a
zero size, which would have set the width to 12 points. No invented metrics.

MEASURED, driving Command comma on iTerm2 3.5.14 with launchd on:
  before   UNRECOGNIZED -[NSStepperCell _stepUpImage], no Preferences
  after    that raise is gone and the decode reaches the NEXT missing thing,
           +[NSFontCollection fontCollectionWithAllAvailableDescriptors]
  2 of 2 runs render CONTENT, so nothing regressed on screen.

PREFERENCES STILL DOES NOT OPEN. This is one link of a chain and the task records the rest,
including the important part: implementing all three known links together makes the whole display
go black, 3 of 3, against a control of 3 of 3 clean without the keystroke. That is why only this
one lands. #257 carries the evidence and the bisect.

